{'name': 'debug_parser_output', 'id': 'toolu_01UXfBHoaQ8J73SvtMLPCdFL', 'arguments': '{"parser_code": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\\n\\"\\"\\"\\nLN6280P Parser - Loan Billing Statement\\n\\nThis parser is designed to handle LN6280P documents, which are standard loan billing\\nstatements, often from platforms like Jack Henry. These documents typically include\\na header with account details, an account activity summary, an explanation of\\nthe amount due, a detailed transaction history, and a payment slip.\\n\\nDocument Type: LN6280P - Loan Billing Statement\\nFormat: Multi-notice batch document with standardized section headers.\\n\\nROBUST PARSING APPROACH:\\n- Uses header keyword detection for section boundaries.\\n- Flexible, multi-pattern extraction for key-value pairs in summary tables.\\n- Specific logic to parse the fixed-column transaction history table.\\n- Tolerant customer address block detection with support for Unicode.\\n- Handles alphanumeric account numbers and varied name formats.\\n\\nDOCUMENT STRUCTURE:\\nThe LN6280P document typically contains:\\n1. Document header with LN6280P identifier\\n2. Multiple loan billing statements in batch format\\n3. Each statement contains:\\n   - Account header with \'001\' marker and \\"Loan Billing Statement\\" title\\n   - Statement date and account number\\n   - Customer name (prefixed with \'3\') and address lines (prefixed with \'1\')\\n   - Account Activity Summary table with financial details\\n   - Explanation of Amount Due section\\n   - Loan Transaction History table\\n   - Payment stub section (prefixed with \'046\')\\n\\nPARSING STRATEGY:\\n- Section boundary detection using \'001\' markers\\n- Customer name extraction using \'3\' prefix patterns\\n- Address parsing with city/state/zip separation\\n- Financial data extraction from two-column summary tables\\n- Transaction history parsing from fixed-width table format\\n- Unicode character support for international names\\n- Alphanumeric account number handling\\n\\"\\"\\"\\n\\nimport sys\\nimport json\\nimport re\\nfrom typing import List, Dict, Optional, Tuple\\n\\ndef find_section_boundaries(lines: List[str]) -> List[int]:\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    Finds the starting line index of each new notice section. For LN6280P,\\n    the most reliable marker is the page start marker \'001\' often followed\\n    by the \\"Loan Billing Statement\\" title.\\n    \\n    Args:\\n        lines: List of all document lines\\n        \\n    Returns:\\n        List of line indices where new sections begin\\n        \\n    Algorithm:\\n    1. Scan through all lines looking for \'001\' prefix markers\\n    2. For each \'001\' found, check the next 2-3 lines for \\"Loan Billing Statement\\"\\n    3. If title is found, mark this as a section boundary\\n    4. Avoid duplicate boundaries in case of false positives\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    boundaries = []\\n    \\n    # Iterate through each line with its index\\n    for i, line in enumerate(lines):\\n        # Check if line starts with \'001\' (Jack Henry page marker)\\n        if line.strip().startswith(\'001\'):\\n            # Confirm it\'s a new notice by checking for the title in the vicinity\\n            # Look ahead up to 3 lines to find the statement title\\n            for j in range(i, min(i + 3, len(lines))):\\n                if \\"Loan Billing Statement\\" in lines[j]:\\n                    # Avoid adding duplicate boundaries\\n                    if i not in boundaries:\\n                        boundaries.append(i)\\n                    break\\n    return boundaries\\n\\ndef extract_document_date(lines: List[str]) -> Optional[str]:\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    Extracts the main document date from the first notice\'s \\"Statement Date\\".\\n    This becomes the overall document date in the metadata section.\\n    \\n    Args:\\n        lines: List of all document lines\\n        \\n    Returns:\\n        Date string in M/D/YY format or None if not found\\n        \\n    Pattern Matching:\\n    - Looks for \\"Statement Date\\" followed by whitespace and a date\\n    - Date format: 1-2 digits / 1-2 digits / 2 digits (M/D/YY)\\n    - Case-insensitive matching\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    for line in lines:\\n        # Search for \\"Statement Date\\" followed by a date pattern\\n        match = re.search(r\'Statement Date\\\\s+(\\\\d{1,2}/\\\\d{1,2}/\\\\d{2})\', line, re.IGNORECASE)\\n        if match:\\n            return match.group(1).strip()\\n    return None\\n\\ndef find_customer_info(lines: List[str]) -> Tuple[Optional[str], List[str]]:\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    Finds customer name and address block using robust patterns.\\n    It supports names with titles, suffixes, and Unicode characters.\\n    \\n    Args:\\n        lines: List of lines from a single notice section\\n        \\n    Returns:\\n        Tuple of (customer_name, address_lines)\\n        \\n    Customer Name Extraction:\\n    - Looks for lines starting with \'3\' (Jack Henry customer name prefix)\\n    - Supports complex names with:\\n      * Titles: DR., PROF., REV., MR., MS., etc.\\n      * Suffixes: JR., SR., III, IV, etc.\\n      * International characters: \\u00c0-\\u00ff (Unicode range)\\n      * Special characters: apostrophes, hyphens, ampersands, periods\\n      * Business entities: LLC, CORP., INC., TRUST, etc.\\n    \\n    Address Extraction:\\n    - Address lines follow customer name\\n    - Lines prefixed with \'1 \' are address components\\n    - Stops when hitting \\"Account Activity Summary\\" section\\n    - Supports multi-line addresses (street, city/state/zip)\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    for i, line in enumerate(lines):\\n        stripped = line.strip()\\n        # Pattern: line starts with \'3\', followed by what looks like a name.\\n        if stripped.startswith(\'3\'):\\n            # This regex captures complex names, including titles, suffixes, hyphens, and Unicode.\\n            # [A-Z\\u00c0-\\u00ff] covers English + extended Latin characters for international names\\n            # .,\\\\\'&\\\\s- covers punctuation commonly found in names and business entities\\n            name_match = re.match(r\'3\\\\s+([A-Z\\u00c0-\\u00ff.,\\\\\'&\\\\s-]+)\', stripped, re.UNICODE | re.IGNORECASE)\\n            if name_match:\\n                # Clean up the customer name by normalizing whitespace\\n                customer_name = \' \'.join(name_match.group(1).strip().split())\\n                address_lines = []\\n                \\n                # Address lines typically follow on subsequent lines.\\n                # Look ahead up to 4 lines for address components\\n                for j in range(i + 1, min(i + 5, len(lines))):\\n                    addr_line = lines[j].strip()\\n                    \\n                    # Remove \'1 \' prefix if present (Jack Henry address line marker)\\n                    if addr_line.startswith(\'1 \'):\\n                        addr_line = addr_line[2:].strip()\\n                    \\n                    # Stop if we hit the next major section header\\n                    if \\"Account Activity Summary\\" in addr_line:\\n                        break\\n                    \\n                    # Add non-empty address lines\\n                    if addr_line:\\n                        address_lines.append(addr_line)\\n                \\n                # A valid customer block needs both name and at least one address line\\n                if customer_name and address_lines:\\n                    return customer_name, address_lines\\n    return None, []\\n\\ndef extract_summary_field(text: str, patterns: List[str]) -> Optional[str]:\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    Extracts a value from summary tables using regex patterns.\\n    It removes leading/trailing whitespace and currency symbols.\\n    \\n    Args:\\n        text: The full section text to search within\\n        patterns: List of regex patterns to try in order\\n        \\n    Returns:\\n        Extracted field value with $ symbols removed, or None\\n        \\n    Field Extraction Strategy:\\n    - Tries multiple regex patterns in sequence (fallback approach)\\n    - Handles variations in spacing and formatting\\n    - Removes currency symbols ($) but preserves commas in numbers\\n    - Returns first successful match from any pattern\\n    - Uses DOTALL and IGNORECASE flags for robust matching\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    for pattern in patterns:\\n        # Use DOTALL to match across line breaks, IGNORECASE for flexibility\\n        match = re.search(pattern, text, re.DOTALL | re.IGNORECASE)\\n        if match:\\n            # Return the first non-empty capture group.\\n            # This allows patterns with multiple optional groups\\n            for group in match.groups():\\n                if group:\\n                    # Strip whitespace and remove $ symbols, but keep commas\\n                    return group.strip().replace(\'$\', \'\')\\n    return None\\n\\ndef parse_transaction_history(lines: List[str]) -> List[Dict[str, Optional[str]]]:\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    Parses the \\"Loan Transaction History\\" table, handling various alignments\\n    and optional amount values.\\n    \\n    Args:\\n        lines: List of lines from a single notice section\\n        \\n    Returns:\\n        List of transaction dictionaries with date, description, amount, balance\\n        \\n    Transaction Table Structure:\\n    - Header: \\"Loan Transaction History\\"\\n    - Column headers: \\"Date\\", \\"Description\\", \\"Amount\\", \\"Balance\\"\\n    - Data rows: Fixed-width columns with optional amount values\\n    - Some transactions may have no amount (balance-only entries)\\n    \\n    Parsing Logic:\\n    1. Find \\"Loan Transaction History\\" section marker\\n    2. Look for column header line with \\"Date\\" and \\"Description\\"\\n    3. Skip separator lines (containing \\"---\\")\\n    4. Parse data rows using regex for column extraction\\n    5. Handle optional line prefixes (digit followed by space)\\n    6. Extract date, description, optional amount, and balance\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    history = []\\n    in_history_section = False\\n    header_found = False\\n    \\n    for line in lines:\\n        # Look for transaction history section start\\n        if \\"Loan Transaction History\\" in line:\\n            in_history_section = True\\n            continue\\n        \\n        # Look for column headers within the section\\n        if in_history_section and \\"Date\\" in line and \\"Description\\" in line:\\n            header_found = True\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Skip lines before we find the header or separator lines\\n        if not header_found or \\"---\\" in line:\\n            continue\\n        \\n        # This improved regex handles optional line prefixes and variable spacing.\\n        # Pattern breakdown:\\n        # ^\\\\s* - start of line with optional whitespace\\n        # (?:\\\\d\\\\s*)? - optional digit prefix (non-capturing group)\\n        # (\\\\d{1,2}/\\\\d{1,2}/\\\\d{2}) - date in M/D/YY format (group 1)\\n        # \\\\s+ - one or more spaces\\n        # ([\\\\w\\\\s-]+?) - description with words, spaces, hyphens (group 2, non-greedy)\\n        # \\\\s+\\\\$?\\\\s* - spaces, optional $, more spaces\\n        # ([\\\\d,.]*)  - optional amount with digits, commas, periods (group 3)\\n        # \\\\s+\\\\$?\\\\s* - spaces, optional $, more spaces  \\n        # ([\\\\d,.]+) - required balance with digits, commas, periods (group 4)\\n        # \\\\s*$ - end of line with optional whitespace\\n        match = re.match(r\'^\\\\s*(?:\\\\d\\\\s*)?(\\\\d{1,2}/\\\\d{1,2}/\\\\d{2})\\\\s+([\\\\w\\\\s-]+?)\\\\s+\\\\$?\\\\s*([\\\\d,.]*)\\\\s+\\\\$?\\\\s*([\\\\d,.]+)\\\\s*$\', line)\\n        if match:\\n            # Handle optional amount field - empty string becomes None\\n            amount_str = match.group(3).strip() or None\\n            \\n            # Build transaction object\\n            history.append({\\n                \\"date\\": match.group(1).strip(),\\n                \\"description\\": match.group(2).strip(),\\n                \\"amount\\": amount_str,\\n                \\"balance\\": match.group(4).strip()\\n            })\\n    return history\\n\\ndef parse_notice_section(section_text: str) -> Optional[Dict]:\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    Parses a single notice section for an LN6280P document.\\n    \\n    Args:\\n        section_text: Raw text of one complete loan statement section\\n        \\n    Returns:\\n        Structured notice dictionary or None if parsing fails\\n        \\n    Section Parsing Process:\\n    1. Extract financial summary fields from two-column tables\\n    2. Parse special instruction and footnote text blocks\\n    3. Extract transaction history table\\n    4. Find and parse customer information\\n    5. Parse address components with city/state/zip separation\\n    6. Build structured notice object following JSON schema\\n    \\n    Field Categories:\\n    - Header fields: statement_date, payment_due_date, account_number\\n    - Financial fields: balances, amounts, fees, interest\\n    - Text fields: special_instructions, past_due_footnote\\n    - Structured data: customer info, address, transactions\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    lines = section_text.split(\'\\\\n\')\\n    details = {}\\n\\n    # Extract all key-value fields from the summary tables.\\n    # These fields come from the \\"Account Activity Summary\\" and \\"Explanation of Amount Due\\" sections\\n    # Each field uses regex patterns to handle spacing and formatting variations\\n    \\n    # Date fields - statement and payment due dates\\n    details[\'statement_date\'] = extract_summary_field(section_text, [r\'Statement Date\\\\s+([\\\\d/]+)\'])\\n    details[\'payment_due_date\'] = extract_summary_field(section_text, [r\'Payment Due Date\\\\s+([\\\\d/]+)\'])\\n    \\n    # Primary amount field - what customer owes\\n    details[\'total_amount_due\'] = extract_summary_field(section_text, [r\'Total Amount Due\\\\s*\\\\$?\\\\s*([,\\\\d]+\\\\.\\\\d{2})\'])\\n    \\n    # Account summary fields from left column of summary table\\n    details[\'current_balance\'] = extract_summary_field(section_text, [r\'Current Balance\\\\s*\\\\$?\\\\s*([,\\\\d]+\\\\.\\\\d{2})\'])\\n    details[\'payments_and_credits\'] = extract_summary_field(section_text, [r\'Payments and Credits\\\\s*\\\\$?\\\\s*([,\\\\d]+\\\\.\\\\d{2})\'])\\n    details[\'advances_and_debits\'] = extract_summary_field(section_text, [r\'Advances and Debits\\\\s*\\\\$?\\\\s*([,\\\\d]+\\\\.\\\\d{2})\'])\\n    details[\'interest_paid_this_year\'] = extract_summary_field(section_text, [r\'Interest Paid this Year\\\\s*\\\\$?\\\\s*([,\\\\d]+\\\\.\\\\d{2})\'])\\n    details[\'past_due_amount\'] = extract_summary_field(section_text, [r\'Past Due Amount\\\\s*\\\\*\\\\s*\\\\$?\\\\s*([,\\\\d]+\\\\.\\\\d{2})\'])\\n    details[\'credit_limit\'] = extract_summary_field(section_text, [r\'Credit Limit\\\\s*\\\\$?\\\\s*([,\\\\d]+\\\\.\\\\d{2})\'])\\n    details[\'available_credit\'] = extract_summary_field(section_text, [r\'Available Credit\\\\s*\\\\$?\\\\s*([,\\\\d]+\\\\.\\\\d{2})\'])\\n    \\n    # Payment breakdown fields from right column of summary table\\n    details[\'regular_payment_amount\'] = extract_summary_field(section_text, [r\'Regular Payment Amount\\\\s*\\\\$?\\\\s*([,\\\\d]+\\\\.\\\\d{2})\'])\\n    details[\'principal_due\'] = extract_summary_field(section_text, [r\'Principal\\\\s*\\\\$?\\\\s*([,\\\\d]+\\\\.\\\\d{2})\'])\\n    details[\'interest_due\'] = extract_summary_field(section_text, [r\'Interest\\\\s*\\\\$?\\\\s*([,\\\\d]+\\\\.\\\\d{2})\'])\\n    details[\'total_late_fees\'] = extract_summary_field(section_text, [r\'Total Late Fees\\\\s*\\\\$?\\\\s*([,\\\\d]+\\\\.\\\\d{2})\'])\\n\\n    # Extract multi-line text blocks and clean them\\n    \\n    # Past due footnote - exact text extraction\\n    footnote_match = re.search(r\'(\\\\*Past due amount includes total late fees owed\\\\.)\', section_text)\\n    details[\'past_due_footnote\'] = footnote_match.group(1).strip() if footnote_match else None\\n    \\n    # Special instructions - multi-line text that may span several lines with line prefixes\\n    instructions_match = re.search(r\'(Please return this portion.*?account\\\\.)\', section_text, re.DOTALL)\\n    if instructions_match:\\n        text = instructions_match.group(1)\\n        # Remove line prefixes like \\"1 \\" that may appear in multi-line text\\n        cleaned_text = re.sub(r\'\\\\s*\\\\n\\\\s*1\\\\s*\', \' \', text).strip()\\n        # Normalize whitespace - convert multiple spaces/newlines to single spaces\\n        details[\'special_instructions\'] = \' \'.join(cleaned_text.split())\\n    else:\\n        details[\'special_instructions\'] = None\\n\\n    # Parse the transaction history table\\n    details[\'transactions\'] = parse_transaction_history(lines)\\n    \\n    # Extract customer information using the specialized function\\n    customer_name, address_lines = find_customer_info(lines)\\n    if not customer_name:\\n        # If we can\'t find customer info, this section is invalid\\n        return None\\n\\n    # Build the base notice structure following the JSON schema\\n    notice = {\\n        \\"notice_type\\": \\"Loan Billing Statement\\",\\n        \\"customer\\": {\\n            \\"name\\": customer_name, \\n            \\"address\\": {\\"street\\": None, \\"city\\": None, \\"state\\": None, \\"zip\\": None}, \\n            \\"extra\\": {}\\n        },\\n        \\"account_number\\": extract_summary_field(section_text, [r\'Account Number\\\\s+(\\\\w+)\']),\\n        \\"details\\": details,\\n        \\"extra\\": {}\\n    }\\n\\n    # Robust address parsing - handles various address formats\\n    if address_lines:\\n        # Join all address lines into a single string for analysis\\n        full_address_str = \\" \\".join(address_lines)\\n        \\n        # Look for city/state/zip pattern in the last address line\\n        # Pattern: [city name] [state abbrev] [zip code]\\n        # Example: \\"ROCHESTER NY 14623\\" or \\"NEW YORK NY 10005-1234\\"\\n        addr_match = re.search(r\'^(.*?)\\\\s+([A-Z]{2})\\\\s+(\\\\d{5}(?:-\\\\d{4})?)$\', address_lines[-1])\\n        if addr_match:\\n            # Remove the last line since we\'re parsing it separately\\n            city_state_zip_line = address_lines.pop()\\n            # Remaining lines form the street address\\n            notice[\\"customer\\"][\\"address\\"][\\"street\\"] = \\" \\".join(address_lines)\\n            # Extract city (remove trailing commas), state, and zip\\n            notice[\\"customer\\"][\\"address\\"][\\"city\\"] = addr_match.group(1).strip().rstrip(\',\')\\n            notice[\\"customer\\"][\\"address\\"][\\"state\\"] = addr_match.group(2).strip()\\n            notice[\\"customer\\"][\\"address\\"][\\"zip\\"] = addr_match.group(3).strip()\\n        else: \\n            # Fallback for single-line address or non-standard format\\n            notice[\\"customer\\"][\\"address\\"][\\"street\\"] = address_lines[0]\\n             \\n    return notice\\n\\ndef parse_ln6280p(raw_text: str) -> Dict:\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    Main parsing function for LN6280P documents.\\n    \\n    Args:\\n        raw_text: Complete raw text content of the LN6280P document\\n        \\n    Returns:\\n        Structured document dictionary following the JSON schema\\n        \\n    Document Processing Flow:\\n    1. Split raw text into individual lines for line-by-line processing\\n    2. Extract document-level metadata (date, type, format info)\\n    3. Find section boundaries to separate individual loan statements\\n    4. Parse each section as a separate notice\\n    5. Assemble final document structure with metadata, bank info, and notices\\n    \\n    Schema Structure:\\n    - metadata: Document type, date, processing info, format flags\\n    - bank: Bank information (mostly empty for this document type)\\n    - notices: Array of individual loan billing statements\\n    - extra: Additional metadata or future extensions\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    # Split into lines preserving original line structure\\n    lines = raw_text.split(\'\\\\n\')\\n    \\n    # Initialize document structure following JSON schema\\n    document = {\\n        \\"metadata\\": {\\n            \\"bank_name\\": \\"\\",  # Not typically present in LN6280P documents\\n            \\"document_type\\": \\"LN6280P\\",\\n            \\"document_date\\": extract_document_date(lines) or \\"\\",  # Use first statement date\\n            \\"form_type\\": \\"Loan Billing Statement\\",\\n            \\"processing_date\\": None,  # Not available in source document\\n            \\"processing_time\\": None,  # Not available in source document\\n            \\"extra\\": {\\n                # Jack Henry format indicators\\n                \\"format_type\\": \\"JH_ABSOLUTE_RELATIVE\\",  # Jack Henry absolute/relative line numbering\\n                \\"line_prefix_format\\": True  # Indicates use of line number prefixes\\n            }\\n        },\\n        \\"bank\\": {\\n            # Bank details are not typically present in LN6280P documents\\n            \\"name\\": \\"\\",\\n            \\"address\\": {},  # Empty object for bank address\\n            \\"phone\\": None,\\n            \\"routing_number\\": None,\\n            \\"extra\\": {}\\n        },\\n        \\"notices\\": [],  # Will be populated with parsed loan statements\\n        \\"extra\\": {}\\n    }\\n\\n    # Find all section boundaries in the document\\n    boundaries = find_section_boundaries(lines)\\n    \\n    # Process each section as a separate loan statement\\n    for i in range(len(boundaries)):\\n        # Calculate section boundaries\\n        start_index = boundaries[i]\\n        end_index = boundaries[i + 1] if i + 1 < len(boundaries) else len(lines)\\n        \\n        # Extract section text for this loan statement\\n        section_text = \'\\\\n\'.join(lines[start_index:end_index])\\n        \\n        # Parse the section if it contains meaningful content\\n        if section_text.strip():\\n            notice = parse_notice_section(section_text)\\n            if notice:\\n                document[\\"notices\\"].append(notice)\\n                \\n    return document\\n\\ndef main():\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    Main entry point for the LN6280P parser script.\\n    \\n    Command Line Usage:\\n        python parser.py <input_file>\\n        \\n    Arguments:\\n        input_file: Path to the LN6280P document text file\\n        \\n    Output:\\n        Structured JSON printed to stdout\\n        Error messages printed to stderr\\n        \\n    Exit Codes:\\n        0: Success\\n        1: Error (file not found, parsing error, etc.)\\n        \\n    Error Handling:\\n    - Validates command line arguments\\n    - Checks file existence\\n    - Handles encoding issues (UTF-8 required for Unicode names)\\n    - Provides meaningful error messages\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    # Validate command line arguments\\n    if len(sys.argv) != 2:\\n        print(\\"Usage: python parser_ln6280p.py <input_file>\\", file=sys.stderr)\\n        sys.exit(1)\\n    \\n    input_file = sys.argv[1]\\n    \\n    try:\\n        # Read input file with UTF-8 encoding to handle international characters\\n        with open(input_file, \'r\', encoding=\'utf-8\') as f:\\n            raw_text = f.read()\\n        \\n        # Parse the document\\n        document = parse_ln6280p(raw_text)\\n        \\n        # Output JSON with proper Unicode handling\\n        # ensure_ascii=False preserves Unicode characters in output\\n        # indent=2 provides readable formatting\\n        print(json.dumps(document, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False))\\n        \\n    except FileNotFoundError:\\n        print(f\\"Error: File \'{input_file}\' not found.\\", file=sys.stderr)\\n        sys.exit(1)\\n    except Exception as e:\\n        print(f\\"An unexpected error occurred: {e}\\", file=sys.stderr)\\n        sys.exit(1)\\n\\n# Script entry point - only run main() when script is executed directly\\nif __name__ == \\"__main__\\":\\n    main()", "input_text": "    LN6280P                                                                                                                                                                                               \\n001                                        Loan Billing Statement                                                                                                                                         \\n   1                                       Billing Date               2/23/25                                                                                                                             \\n   1                                       Page 001                                                                                                                                                       \\n   1                                       Account Number             1111111                                                                                                                             \\n   1                                       Due Date                  11/09/10                                                                                                                             \\n   1                                       Phone Number              (555) 123-4567                                                                                                                      \\n   2                                    **Payment Amount Due** $     11,986.81                                                                                                                             \\n   4 MICKEY MOUSE                                                                                                                                                                                    \\n   1 1234 MOUSE DRIV